                <h2>What Problem Does Multi-Account Tiering Actually Solve?</h2>
<p>Many overseas teams start experiencing "unclear responsibilities and expenses" once they exceed 10 accounts. The essence of multi-account tiering is to clarify boundaries of authority and responsibility, making financial reconciliation and ad risk control traceable.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q1: When Does a Team Need Account Tiering?</h2>
<p><strong>The standard isn't account count, but whether "account managers" exceed 2 people.</strong></p>
<p>If only 1 operator manages 5 accounts, tiering is unnecessary—they know the situation. But if 3 operators each manage a batch of accounts, with finance needing reconciliation and executives reviewing ROI, tiering becomes essential. Simply put: <strong>tiering is needed whenever "money managers" and "spenders" are different people.</strong></p>
<hr />
<h2>Q2: What Are the Standards for Account Tiering?</h2>
<p>Recommend tiering by <strong>campaign objectives</strong> and <strong>risk levels</strong>:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Tier</th>
<th>Typical Characteristics</th>
<th>Typical Scenarios</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Core Accounts</strong></td>
<td>High budget, daily spend over $10K, main product lines</td>
<td>New product launches, bestseller scaling</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Testing Accounts</strong></td>
<td>Small budget, multiple creatives, new market exploration</td>
<td>Southeast Asian market testing, feature testing</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Brand Accounts</strong></td>
<td>Long-term campaigns, brand protection</td>
<td>Brand keyword bidding, official account operations</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Backup/Isolated Accounts</strong></td>
<td>Risk hedging, creative isolation</td>
<td>Backup after primary account suspension</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>After tiering, each tier can have different budget approval processes and creative review standards, avoiding efficiency losses from "one-size-fits-all" approaches.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q3: How to Allocate Budget Across Tiers?</h2>
<p><strong>Principle: Core accounts protect ROI ceiling, testing accounts protect exploration floor.</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Core accounts: Higher daily budget limits (e.g., $5,000-$10,000), but ROI thresholds must be enforced</li>
<li>Testing accounts: Daily budget capped at $500; upgrade to core tier only after positive models are found</li>
<li>Brand accounts: Fixed monthly budgets, adjusted as needed, not subject to ROI evaluation</li>
</ul>
<p>One utility app team's actual practice: Core accounts take 70% of total budget, testing accounts 20%, brand accounts 10%. Testing accounts require click-through rates above 2% before applying for upgrades.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q4: How to Design Team Permissions After Tiering?</h2>
<p>Permission design must match organizational structure—it's a "management issue," not a "technical issue." Recommend a separation-of-powers model:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Operational Rights</strong>: Advertisers can operate ad groups and adjust bids, but cannot modify budget caps</li>
<li><strong>Financial Rights</strong>: Finance staff control account top-ups and view spending, but cannot log into ad backends</li>
<li><strong>Approval Rights</strong>: Operations directors or executives approve budget adjustments, account additions, or suspensions</li>
</ol>
<p>This ensures no single person can complete the closed loop of "top-up + spend + withdrawal," providing stronger risk control.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q5: How to Handle Financial Reconciliation After Tiering?</h2>
<p>One core value of tiering is <strong>reconciliation efficiency.</strong> Recommend establishing reconciliation ledgers by tier:</p>
<ul>
<li>Core accounts: Daily reconciliation, focused on monitoring ROI fluctuations</li>
<li>Testing accounts: Weekly reconciliation, aggregating CTR and conversion costs</li>
<li>Brand accounts: Monthly reconciliation, tracking brand keyword search share changes</li>
</ul>
<p>During reconciliation, focus on comparing three data points: <strong>platform backend spend, top-up records, and conversion data.</strong> Any inconsistencies must be investigated within 48 hours.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q6: Where Are the Risk Control Focus Areas for Tiered Management?</h2>
<p>Multi-account advertising's biggest fears are <strong>account bans</strong> and <strong>abnormal spending.</strong></p>
<p><strong>应对账户被封:</strong><br />
- Don't expose core accounts naked: Ensure domains, creatives, and landing pages are fully compliant<br />
- Keep backup accounts active: Long-inactive accounts risk platform "zombie account" flags<br />
- Establish account health alerts: Any single account's daily spend dropping over 30% requires investigation</p>
<p><strong>应对异常消耗:</strong><br />
- Set single-account daily spending caps with automatic pauses when exceeded<br />
- Enable low balance alerts, pushing notifications when thresholds are reached<br />
- Prohibit operators from logging into accounts on public WiFi</p>
<hr />
<h2>One-Line Summary</h2>
<p>Account tiering isn't simply "grouping accounts"—it's a management mechanism that aligns <strong>responsibilities, budgets, and risk controls.</strong> The ultimate goal is enabling teams to clearly explain, even during scale expansion: where the money went, who approved it, and what the results were.</p>
